<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shah Nimatullah Wali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1330">1330</a>–<a href="/years/1431">1431</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Nur ad-Din Nimatullah Wali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shah Nimatullah Wali was a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> master and poet, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, who settled at Mahan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where his shrine stands. He founded the Nimatullahi order, one of the major Sufi orders of Iran. The Bahmani sultan Ahmad Shah I became his disciple, and members of his family moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, where they were honoured at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-nimatullah-wali.jpg" alt="Shah Nimatullah Wali" width="281" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Shah_Nematollah_Vali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
